From 47254e9b53e6c0d4d6551f8bbd4b134fdcf9e7c5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr233 <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:53:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 77/79] vgpu: read EXEC_INDIRECT3's validity ops from the low
 nibble

Component: FIFO decode, EXEC_INDIRECT3 and EXEC_INDIRECT_INFO resource
tables.

0075 widened each eight-byte 0x43 record by copying its flag word into
the four validity-op bytes of a 0x37 record. The guest packs the four
ops into the low nibble of the flag word's first byte (clear host, set
host, clear guest, set guest, from bit 0) and also sets the high nibble.
Read as four bytes, any high-nibble bit said "clear host valid", which
this device takes to mean the guest's CPU wrote the resource. Nearly
every resource of every submission said so, so every frame a pass had
just stored into a linear texture was abandoned before anything read
it.

One 27.2 boot's tables, by first byte: 0x40 572, 0x20 313, 0x10 276,
0xa0 268, 0x42 173, 0xa8 157, 0xd0 102. The low nibble agrees with what
0x37 says: render targets and kernel outputs carry 0x02, set host valid,
and 0x01 alone is rare. The widening now unpacks the nibble.

Measured on arm64 Linux (NVIDIA GB10), macOS 27.2 guest at protocol 237,
cold boots:
- before: 23 of 25 armed GVA writeback debts abandoned as guest-written,
  531 validity generation bumps, and the Dock's backdrop blur kernel
  read an all-zero 896x64 texture, so the Dock drew with no background;
- after: no debt abandoned (of 79, 38 paid and 39 superseded), 4 generation
  bumps, and the Dock's Liquid Glass background back.
What the high nibble means is not established.

Verified: the reims-vgpu test suite passes; clippy unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
